        place: '[Cologne]: the book names neither place nor printer. The catalogues supply Cologne and Johannes Soter, the Library of Congress and Eton with a query, and Eton’s record notes Adams’s suggestion of Basel. Four or five issues dated 1533 are reported; apart from the Library of Congress and McGill copies used here, they have not been compared.',
        locator: 'Book III, chapter 30, “Alius characterizandi modus, à Cabalistis traditus”: the table captioned “Scriptura Cœlestis” on p. CCLXXIII (misprinted CCXLXXIII; signed z iii) of the Library of Congress copy, with the same page in the McGill copy. In the English of 1651, “CHAP. XXX. Another manner of making Characters, delivered by Cabalists.”, the table “Celestiall writing.” is on p. 439.',
        notes: 'The earliest printing the sources document. Agrippa had sent a draft of the work to Trithemius, whose reply is dated 8 April 1510, but whether that draft already held this chapter and its tables is not known. Vittoria Perrone Compagni’s critical edition (Leiden: Brill, 1992), which takes account of the draft, is the place to settle it. A blog report (Cipher Mysteries, 2009) that the River alphabet of Panteo’s Voarchadumia (Venice, 1530) combines all three of Agrippa’s alphabets would, if true, put Celestial-like signs in print three years earlier; it is known only at second hand, and the plate has not been compared. Dates of 1531 confuse the book with the printings of that year, which hold Book I only.',
