        follows: 'Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres ([Cologne], July 1533), Book III, chapter 30, “Alius characterizandi modus, à Cabalistis traditus”: the table captioned “Scriptura Cœlestis”, p. CCLXXIII (misprinted CCXLXXIII in this setting), sig. z3r (“z iii”), in the Library of Congress copy, Rare Book and Special Collections Division, BF1598.A3 O4 1533 (LCCN 20007812), digitised as loc.gov resource rbc.2009gen12345, image 277.',
        drawn: 'Each sign is traced from that page as the centre line of its printed strokes, with a ring wherever the print has one, centred on the printed ring, and every stroke stopped at its ring’s edge. The drawing keeps what the cut shows: which ends carry rings and which are plain, where one stroke meets another without crossing it, the slant and length of each stroke, and the size and place of each sign against the others. The rest is the site’s convention: one even pen for every stroke, where the cutter’s line swells and thins, and every ring drawn at the site’s house size, larger than the printed rings, so that it stays open at text size. Hairlines, specks and blemishes of the impression are left out. Where the Library of Congress impression is faint or broken — Iod’s lower right ring, the right half of Kuff’s arch — the line is taken from the McGill and Jung copies, which print the same cut, and the letter’s note says so.',
        variants: 'Compared sign by sign with two other copies of 1533 and two of the English of 1651. The McGill copy (Osler Library, Bibliotheca Osleriana 1746; Internet Archive, leaf n288) shares the Library of Congress typesetting; the Jung copy (C. G. Jung’s library, e-rara 10.3931/e-rara-4309) is a different setting of the page, its labels without full stops and its Shin spelled Schin. Laid over the Library of Congress signs, both print the same cut: no stroke or ring differs. The English of 1651 (Wellcome Collection EPB/B/10377, p. 439, and a second copy digitised at the Internet Archive whose holding library is not stated; the two agree throughout) re-cut the table in rows of seven, seven and eight, still running from right to left and still without numbers or final forms: its strokes are heavier and its rings larger, several signs are a little narrower or squatter, Gimel gains a third ring at the foot of its upright, and Ain’s short stroke slants instead of standing upright. Later copies were cut or engraved again — Barrett’s plate of 1801 among them — and none of them, nor any modern font or chart, is used as a base.',
