    links: [
        { to: 'hebrew', relation: 'Its twenty-two signs stand one for one for the Hebrew letters, in Hebrew order; the numbers and the transliteration on this page are the Hebrew hall’s.' },
        { to: 'malachim', relation: 'The second alphabet of III.30. In 1533 its caption stands at the foot of the Celestial’s page and its table on the next; in 1651 it is on p. 440. In II.51 Agrippa calls the Malachim “the true Characters of the heavens” (J.F., 1651, p. 316).' },
        { to: 'transitus-fluvii', relation: 'The third alphabet of III.30, which de Balmes had printed at Venice in 1523; in 1651 it is on p. 441.' },
        { to: 'theban', relation: 'Printed in the chapter before, III.29: in the Library of Congress copy of 1533 the Theban table and the Celestial stand on the same page. Barrett’s plate sets the Theban above the Celestial.' },
        { to: 'aiq-bekar', relation: 'The cipher of the nine chambers follows the three alphabets in the same chapter, III.30.' },
        { to: 'charakteres', relation: 'The ring-ended signs of ancient and medieval magic, to which the Celestial belongs by its look; the descent is a matter of resemblance, not of a documented derivation.' },
        { to: 'enochian', relation: 'Also called celestial or angelic in modern usage, but a separate alphabet, recorded by John Dee in 1583.' },
        { to: 'agrippa', relation: 'The author and the book through which the Celestial reached later readers.' },
        { to: 'the-books', relation: 'The printed books that carried it — Agrippa 1533, the English of 1651 and Barrett 1801 — and those around it: Duret, who is reported to print it, and Gaffarel, whose celestial writing is a different one.' },
    ],
    cautions: [
        'The signs shown are drawings traced from one printing, the Library of Congress copy of 1533, with the site’s even pen and rings: they keep the printed strokes and their places, not the cutter’s line. Where a drawing departs from the print, the letter’s note or the account of how the letters are drawn says so, and the page itself is shown beside them.',
        'Agrippa reports the alphabet as a writing of the Hebrews and gives it no use. It should not be called his invention, nor an alphabet for speaking with angels.',
        'Keep it apart from three other things: Dee and Kelley’s Enochian, which is also called celestial or angelic; Gaffarel’s constellation-letters of 1629, a different celestial writing; and the star-letter idea that a bookseller’s description credits to Postel (1538), which has not been checked.',
        'Book III first appeared in July 1533; dates of 1531 belong to the printings of Book I alone.',
        'Every later table was cut or engraved again — the English of 1651, Barrett in 1801, and a Latin printing reported as of 1550 whose edition and date have not been confirmed — so the forms may drift from one to the next. This page names the printing it follows.',
        'The tables run from right to left, as Hebrew is read. That is the layout of a printed table, not proof of a direction of writing; modern sites disagree about the direction.',
        'The translator of 1651 signs himself J.F. The EEBO-TCP record calls him probably John French; other names given for him are less secure.',
        'Still uncertain, because the sources that would settle them have not been examined for this page: whether the 1510 draft holds this chapter; whether the other issues of 1533 differ; Duret’s p. 119; the number and caption of Barrett’s plate; the report on Panteo; and which printing Peterson’s font follows, and how far its licence reaches.',
    ],
